<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu of Balhae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 719–<a href="/years/737">737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Dae Muye, King Mu of Balhae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu, personal name Dae Muye, was the second king of Balhae, reigning from 719 to 737, and son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>. He expanded the kingdom over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and used his own reign title. His attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heishui-mohe">Heishui Mohe</a> led to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, including a naval raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 732, and he also opened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 727.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tae Muye was the eldest son of Tae Choyŏng, the founder of the kingdom of Balhae, and came to the throne on his father's death in 719. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Tang Emperor Xuanzong</a> gave him the title of King of the Kyeru Province, and he in turn gave his father the posthumous title King Go. He then declared the era name Inan, an act of independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, yet he also sent frequent embassies to the Tang court, among them his own sons and brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The aggressive expansion of Balhae under King Mu caused friction with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, with Silla in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a>, the Xi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heishui-mohe">Heishui Mohe</a> to the north of Balhae came under the direct control of the Tang in 727, he attacked them, fearing that his kingdom would be caught in a pincer between two enemies.</p><p>In 732 he sent the Balhae navy under Chang Munhyu against Tang territory in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. The assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a>, in which the local governor Wei Jun was killed, was largely an act of piracy, but the governor's death turned it into an international conflict. In the same year the king led troops to Madushan near Shanhaiguan and occupied the towns nearby. In 733 Emperor Xuanzong ordered Tae Munye to attack Balhae together with forces from Silla, but the allied advance was held up by heavy snow and the attack was repelled.</p><p>From 727 Balhae also sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, so as not to stand alone among its neighbours. The king's letter to the court of Japan declared that Balhae had recovered the lands of Goguryeo and carried on the culture of Buyeo, and Japan, whose relations with Silla were strained, welcomed the mission as a revival of Goguryeo. Mu was succeeded in 737 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mun-of-balhae">Dae Heummu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mu of Balhae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dae Joyeong's victory at Tianmenling in 698 allowed the state to form, and under his son King Mu (<b>Dae Muye</b>, r. 719–737) it expanded aggressively.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=728" aria-label="Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7715 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mu of Balhae"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8165 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8165 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=728">What was here in 728 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
